---
import Chapter from '../Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../Figure.astro';
import FigMigration from '../figures/FigMigration.astro';
---

<Chapter id="migration" num="09" kicker="Post-quantum migration" title="Migrating becomes a number on an edge.">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      As each domain migrates at its own pace, classical, hybrid and post-quantum domains will
      coexist for years. Because <strong>one algorithm is one node</strong>, a mixed path can be
      read on the graph hop by hop, and its cost is computed with no extra mechanism.
    </p>
    <p>
      A CA that changes algorithm occupies two nodes, linked by the certificates of RFC 9810: new
      with old, and old with new. Each is an edge with its own cost, so the asymmetry of migration,
      usually described in prose, becomes measurable.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure num={9} caption="For a hybrid receiver, entering the post-quantum node costs 0.266 and is admissible; going back to the classical one is not even in its search space.">
    <FigMigration />
  </Figure>

  <p class="key">
    A receiver governs its own migration by raising <span class="math">τ<sub>d</sub></span>, without reissuing a single
    certificate.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="small muted">
      Using ML-DSA certificates does not make the channel post-quantum. The thesis acts on the
      certificates and on the path decision; the key exchange and the handshake signatures of the
      channel are separate layers.
    </p>
  </div>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .muted {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
